preConditionHook:
// THE STOP RUN THAT IS RECLAIMED, entered on a stop. The PRIOR bar traded
// below the most recent confirmed swing low -- running the protective stops
// that rest there -- but CLOSED back above it, and this bar now takes out
// that prior bar's high by entryTick ticks. The short is the mirror above a
// swing high.
//
// THE STOP TRIGGER IS THE POINT. The breach-and-reclaim half alone is
// FailedSwingBreakoutReversal, which tests the CURRENT bar; this rule tests
// it on bar 1 and adds the entry trigger Brooks always attaches -- a stop one
// tick beyond the signal bar's extreme. No pane-AND reproduces that, because
// every entry pane evaluates its rules on the SAME bar: a placement of
// FailedSwingBreakoutReversal AND a prior-bar-high breakout would test the
// reclaim on bar 0, not bar 1. The one-bar offset and the tick trigger are
// the content, and the tick is why the rule was held for this list.
//
// Not StopRunReversalBar either: its level is a WINDOW extreme
// read one bar before the breaking bar, with no pivot-confirmation lag, and it has no
// entry trigger.
//
// THE SOURCE FRAMES THIS as adding to, or re-entering, a swing position
// (multi-unit language). The mechanism -- breach, reclaim, stop entry --
// needs no second unit, so it is mechanized as an ordinary single-position
// Entry and the 'add to an already-open position' framing is dropped.
//
// THE PIVOT SCAN IS INLINED from rules/SwingHigh.json, the corpus's measured
// primitive, exactly as the B-list decision says to (inline it; do not
// reference the rule file). The twin reads the same pivot as a PRICE through
// SwingLow / SwingHigh at occurrence 1, which IS measured. Measured pivot
// rule (EL_Swing_Probe.txt / el_swing_output1.txt, @ES 60 min): the pivot bar
// is AT OR BEYOND the swingStrength OLDER bars and STRICTLY BEYOND the
// swingStrength NEWER ones -- bars-ago indexing makes the smaller index the
// newer bar, so the strict half is the k < j loop (el_lt / el_gt) and the
// at-or-beyond half the k > j loop (el_le / el_ge); the confirmation lag is
// exactly swingStrength bars, so the pivot is always at least swingStrength
// bars old and can never be bar 1 itself -- the rule is lookahead-safe and
// the pivot the breach refers to already existed; candidate offsets run
// 0..Length-1 inclusive so j stops at lookbackBars - 1, while the
// neighbourhood test reaches swingStrength bars PAST the window, making
// lookbackBars - 1 + swingStrength the deepest bar touched; the -1 SENTINEL
// is guarded through haveLo / haveHi because -1 as a price sits under every
// real price; the comparisons carry the 2.22e-12 relational tolerance; the
// low side's tie sense is the mirror of the measured high-side rule. No
// WFSafe_SwingHigh exists, so plain EL governs.
//
// The tick is ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale(), division-first, both VERIFIED
// and per-symbol constant for the run.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a
// redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is
// fully assigned each bar before it is read.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
pivS = (int)swingStrength;
pivW = (int)lookbackBars;
swLo = -1.0;
for (int j = pivS; j <= pivW - 1; ++j) {
    bool ok = true;
    for (int k = j - pivS; k < j; ++k) {
        if (!el_lt(low[j], low[k])) { ok = false; break; }
    }
    if (ok) {
        for (int k = j + 1; k <= j + pivS; ++k) {
            if (!el_le(low[j], low[k])) { ok = false; break; }
        }
    }
    if (ok) { swLo = low[j]; break; }
}
swHi = -1.0;
for (int j = pivS; j <= pivW - 1; ++j) {
    bool ok = true;
    for (int k = j - pivS; k < j; ++k) {
        if (!el_gt(high[j], high[k])) { ok = false; break; }
    }
    if (ok) {
        for (int k = j + 1; k <= j + pivS; ++k) {
            if (!el_ge(high[j], high[k])) { ok = false; break; }
        }
    }
    if (ok) { swHi = high[j]; break; }
}
haveLo = (swLo != -1.0);
haveHi = (swHi != -1.0);
